1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,175
Sweetheart, I just saw a Christmas centerpiece

2
00:00:03,175 --> 00:00:06,350
in a department store for eighty-nine dollars.

3
00:00:06,530 --> 00:00:08,277
A few pine branches,

4
00:00:08,277 --> 00:00:11,072
some pinecones, three red balls,

5
00:00:11,072 --> 00:00:12,207
and a candle.

6
00:00:12,387 --> 00:00:13,978
Eighty-nine dollars!

7
00:00:14,158 --> 00:00:16,692
My mother made one every single week

8
00:00:16,692 --> 00:00:18,452
of December, for nothing,

9
00:00:18,452 --> 00:00:20,915
from what she found in our backyard

10
00:00:20,915 --> 00:00:23,167
and the woods behind Elm Street.

11
00:00:23,347 --> 00:00:25,217
And hers were prettier.

12
00:00:25,397 --> 00:00:27,504
Tonight I'm going to show you how

13
00:00:27,504 --> 00:00:28,206
she did it.

14
00:00:28,557 --> 00:00:31,236
So tonight I'm giving you nine Christmas

15
00:00:31,236 --> 00:00:33,848
centerpieces you can make from your own

16
00:00:33,848 --> 00:00:35,791
backyard, for almost nothing,

17
00:00:35,791 --> 00:00:38,537
and the three mistakes that make homemade

18
00:00:38,537 --> 00:00:39,609
ones look cheap.

19
00:00:39,789 --> 00:00:40,906
And stay with me,

20
00:00:40,906 --> 00:00:43,073
because number nine is the one my

21
00:00:43,073 --> 00:00:45,962
mother made for Christmas dinner every year,

22
00:00:45,962 --> 00:00:48,523
and the minister's wife asked to borrow

23
00:00:48,523 --> 00:00:50,099
it for the church altar.

24
00:00:50,449 --> 00:00:52,052
In my mother's day,

25
00:00:52,052 --> 00:00:55,766
honey, nobody bought decorations at a store.

26
00:00:55,946 --> 00:00:58,180
You went outside with a pair of

27
00:00:58,180 --> 00:00:59,766
scissors and a basket.

28
00:00:59,946 --> 00:01:01,791
Pine branches, holly,

29
00:01:01,791 --> 00:01:04,780
cedar, red berries off the bushes,

30
00:01:04,780 --> 00:01:06,713
pinecones, bare twigs,

31
00:01:06,713 --> 00:01:10,493
dried hydrangeas, even weeds from the ditch

32
00:01:10,493 --> 00:01:13,306
that had gone to seed and looked

33
00:01:13,306 --> 00:01:14,888
like little stars.

34
00:01:15,068 --> 00:01:16,248
It was all free,

35
00:01:16,248 --> 00:01:18,904
and the house smelled like the woods

36
00:01:18,904 --> 00:01:19,863
all December.

37
00:01:20,213 --> 00:01:21,724
Before you start,

38
00:01:21,724 --> 00:01:23,858
here's what to look for.

39
00:01:24,038 --> 00:01:25,559
Anything evergreen.

40
00:01:25,739 --> 00:01:27,713
Anything with berries.

41
00:01:27,893 --> 00:01:30,040
Pinecones of every size.

42
00:01:30,220 --> 00:01:33,506
Interesting bare branches with a nice shape.

43
00:01:33,686 --> 00:01:34,941
And seed pods,

44
00:01:34,941 --> 00:01:37,808
like milkweed or sweetgum balls.

45
00:01:37,988 --> 00:01:40,752
Just ask permission if it isn't your

46
00:01:40,752 --> 00:01:43,210
land, and never take more than a

47
00:01:43,210 --> 00:01:45,360
few branches from one plant.

48
00:01:45,540 --> 00:01:47,304
In a minute I'll tell you the

49
00:01:47,304 --> 00:01:49,859
biggest mistake people make with greenery.

50
00:01:50,209 --> 00:01:53,054
Danny and I loved gathering day,

51
00:01:53,054 --> 00:01:53,587
honey.

52
00:01:53,767 --> 00:01:55,975
The first Saturday in December,

53
00:01:55,975 --> 00:01:58,112
my mother would hand us each a

54
00:01:58,112 --> 00:02:00,533
basket and a pair of old scissors,

55
00:02:00,533 --> 00:02:02,528
and send us out to the woods

56
00:02:02,528 --> 00:02:03,810
behind Elm Street.

57
00:02:03,990 --> 00:02:06,853
Whoever found the biggest pinecone got to

58
00:02:06,853 --> 00:02:08,599
put the star on the tree.

59
00:02:08,779 --> 00:02:10,927
Danny cheated every year.

60
00:02:11,107 --> 00:02:14,223
He'd bring one from the Hendersons' yard,

61
00:02:14,223 --> 00:02:15,515
across the creek,

62
00:02:15,515 --> 00:02:17,643
where they had a giant pine.

63
00:02:17,823 --> 00:02:19,414
I never told on him.

64
00:02:19,764 --> 00:02:22,925
And bring your pinecones and branches inside

65
00:02:22,925 --> 00:02:23,931
the right way,

66
00:02:23,931 --> 00:02:24,721
sweetheart.

67
00:02:24,901 --> 00:02:27,397
Shake them hard outside first.

68
00:02:27,577 --> 00:02:29,873
Then bake the pinecones on a cookie

69
00:02:29,873 --> 00:02:32,431
sheet at two hundred degrees for thirty

70
00:02:32,431 --> 00:02:34,595
minutes, to get rid of any little

71
00:02:34,595 --> 00:02:36,563
bugs or spiders hiding inside.

72
00:02:36,743 --> 00:02:38,111
As they warm up,

73
00:02:38,111 --> 00:02:39,736
they open up wider,

74
00:02:39,736 --> 00:02:42,130
too, and look even prettier.

75
00:02:42,310 --> 00:02:45,004
Your kitchen will smell like a forest.

76
00:02:45,354 --> 00:02:47,083
Mistake number one.

77
00:02:47,263 --> 00:02:48,900
Making it too tall.

78
00:02:49,080 --> 00:02:51,417
A centerpiece on a dinner table has

79
00:02:51,417 --> 00:02:53,553
to be low enough that people can

80
00:02:53,553 --> 00:02:55,222
see each other across it.

81
00:02:55,402 --> 00:02:57,354
If you have to peek around it

82
00:02:57,354 --> 00:02:58,767
to pass the potatoes,

83
00:02:58,767 --> 00:02:59,709
it's too tall.

84
00:02:59,889 --> 00:03:02,172
The rule is no higher than your

85
00:03:02,172 --> 00:03:04,454
elbow when you're sitting down,

86
00:03:04,454 --> 00:03:05,927
about twelve inches.

87
00:03:06,107 --> 00:03:08,634
Save the tall arrangements for the mantel

88
00:03:08,634 --> 00:03:09,682
or the sideboard.

89
00:03:10,033 --> 00:03:11,855
Mistake number two.

90
00:03:12,035 --> 00:03:13,382
Dry greenery.

91
00:03:13,562 --> 00:03:15,904
Branches cut and left out in a

92
00:03:15,904 --> 00:03:18,480
warm house dry out in three days,

93
00:03:18,480 --> 00:03:21,447
drop needles all over your tablecloth,

94
00:03:21,447 --> 00:03:22,618
and turn brown.

95
00:03:22,798 --> 00:03:24,266
Before you use them,

96
00:03:24,266 --> 00:03:26,690
soak the cut branches in a bucket

97
00:03:26,690 --> 00:03:28,452
of cool water overnight.

98
00:03:28,632 --> 00:03:31,267
Then mist them every couple of days.

99
00:03:31,447 --> 00:03:34,089
And mistake number three is too many

100
00:03:34,089 --> 00:03:35,337
different things.

101
00:03:35,517 --> 00:03:37,351
Pick three materials.

102
00:03:37,531 --> 00:03:39,351
Green, a color,

103
00:03:39,351 --> 00:03:41,049
and a texture.

104
00:03:41,229 --> 00:03:42,402
That's all.

105
00:03:42,752 --> 00:03:45,271
Number one, and the easiest.

106
00:03:45,451 --> 00:03:46,949
The pinecone bowl.

107
00:03:47,129 --> 00:03:49,776
Take your prettiest wooden bowl or a

108
00:03:49,776 --> 00:03:50,879
big glass bowl,

109
00:03:50,879 --> 00:03:53,746
and fill it with pinecones of different

110
00:03:53,746 --> 00:03:54,187
sizes.

111
00:03:54,367 --> 00:03:56,570
Tuck a few sprigs of cedar or

112
00:03:56,570 --> 00:03:58,013
holly between them.

113
00:03:58,193 --> 00:04:00,937
Add a handful of red berries or

114
00:04:00,937 --> 00:04:02,442
a few red apples.

115
00:04:02,622 --> 00:04:04,271
Done in two minutes.

116
00:04:04,451 --> 00:04:06,577
If you want a little sparkle,

117
00:04:06,577 --> 00:04:09,215
brush the tips of the pinecones with

118
00:04:09,215 --> 00:04:11,561
white glue and dip them in salt,

119
00:04:11,561 --> 00:04:13,100
so they look frosted.

120
00:04:13,451 --> 00:04:14,554
Number two.

121
00:04:14,734 --> 00:04:15,813
The Yule log.

122
00:04:15,993 --> 00:04:17,174
Find a short,

123
00:04:17,174 --> 00:04:20,442
fat log, about eighteen inches long,

124
00:04:20,442 --> 00:04:22,077
from the woodpile.

125
00:04:22,257 --> 00:04:24,459
Ask your husband to cut a flat

126
00:04:24,459 --> 00:04:26,807
side on the bottom so it doesn't

127
00:04:26,807 --> 00:04:29,302
roll, and drill three holes in the

128
00:04:29,302 --> 00:04:30,477
top for candles.

129
00:04:30,657 --> 00:04:34,093
Tuck greenery and pinecones around the base.

130
00:04:34,273 --> 00:04:37,211
It looks like something from a Christmas

131
00:04:37,211 --> 00:04:39,854
card, honey, and it costs nothing at

132
00:04:39,854 --> 00:04:40,148
all.

133
00:04:40,328 --> 00:04:42,603
Ours lasted twenty years.

134
00:04:42,954 --> 00:04:44,687
If this is helping you,

135
00:04:44,687 --> 00:04:46,722
tap the like button for me,

136
00:04:46,722 --> 00:04:47,551
sweetheart.

137
00:04:47,731 --> 00:04:50,506
It helps Grandma more than you know.

138
00:04:50,686 --> 00:04:52,033
Number three.

139
00:04:52,213 --> 00:04:54,639
Mason jars down the middle of the

140
00:04:54,639 --> 00:04:55,080
table.

141
00:04:55,260 --> 00:04:57,992
Fill three or five jars with water,

142
00:04:57,992 --> 00:05:00,490
and put a few sprigs of greenery

143
00:05:00,490 --> 00:05:02,363
and berries in each one,

144
00:05:02,363 --> 00:05:04,003
like little bouquets.

145
00:05:04,183 --> 00:05:06,577
Or put a little battery candle in

146
00:05:06,577 --> 00:05:08,972
each jar, nestled in a handful of

147
00:05:08,972 --> 00:05:11,947
cranberries or Epsom salt that looks like

148
00:05:11,947 --> 00:05:12,310
snow.

149
00:05:12,660 --> 00:05:14,813
And a word about candles,

150
00:05:14,813 --> 00:05:15,330
honey.

151
00:05:15,510 --> 00:05:18,751
Real candles near dry greenery are a

152
00:05:18,751 --> 00:05:20,282
real fire danger.

153
00:05:20,462 --> 00:05:23,129
Never leave them burning when you leave

154
00:05:23,129 --> 00:05:25,385
the room, and keep the flame well

155
00:05:25,385 --> 00:05:26,684
above the branches.

156
00:05:26,864 --> 00:05:28,469
Or do what I do now,

157
00:05:28,469 --> 00:05:31,598
and use the little battery candles with

158
00:05:31,598 --> 00:05:32,240
a timer.

159
00:05:32,420 --> 00:05:34,565
They flicker like the real thing,

160
00:05:34,565 --> 00:05:36,451
they're safe in a pile of dry

161
00:05:36,451 --> 00:05:38,791
pine, and you never have to remember

162
00:05:38,791 --> 00:05:39,897
to blow them out.

163
00:05:40,247 --> 00:05:41,338
Number four.

164
00:05:41,518 --> 00:05:43,120
The greenery runner.

165
00:05:43,300 --> 00:05:45,410
Instead of one centerpiece,

166
00:05:45,410 --> 00:05:47,520
lay a long line of cedar or

167
00:05:47,520 --> 00:05:50,489
pine branches right down the center of

168
00:05:50,489 --> 00:05:52,287
the table, overlapping,

169
00:05:52,287 --> 00:05:53,459
like a garland.

170
00:05:53,639 --> 00:05:55,196
Tuck in pinecones,

171
00:05:55,196 --> 00:05:58,828
oranges, and little candles along the way.

172
00:05:59,008 --> 00:06:00,506
It looks so rich,

173
00:06:00,506 --> 00:06:03,060
honey, like a magazine table.

174
00:06:03,240 --> 00:06:04,400
And it's low,

175
00:06:04,400 --> 00:06:06,363
so everybody can talk.

176
00:06:06,543 --> 00:06:09,390
That's what I use for Christmas dinner

177
00:06:09,390 --> 00:06:09,690
now.

178
00:06:10,040 --> 00:06:11,363
Number five.

179
00:06:11,543 --> 00:06:14,367
A big bowl of oranges and clove

180
00:06:14,367 --> 00:06:15,096
oranges.

181
00:06:15,276 --> 00:06:17,760
Stud a few oranges with cloves,

182
00:06:17,760 --> 00:06:19,443
in swirls or stripes,

183
00:06:19,443 --> 00:06:21,126
leave the rest plain,

184
00:06:21,126 --> 00:06:23,370
and pile them in a bowl with

185
00:06:23,370 --> 00:06:26,015
sprigs of bay leaves or rosemary.

186
00:06:26,195 --> 00:06:29,263
It's beautiful, and the whole dining room

187
00:06:29,263 --> 00:06:30,909
smells like Christmas.

188
00:06:31,089 --> 00:06:33,101
And at the end of the week,

189
00:06:33,101 --> 00:06:34,815
you eat the plain ones.

190
00:06:34,995 --> 00:06:37,373
My mother called it the centerpiece you

191
00:06:37,373 --> 00:06:38,653
can have for dessert.

192
00:06:39,003 --> 00:06:41,379
So far, the pinecone bowl,

193
00:06:41,379 --> 00:06:43,206
the log, mason jars,

194
00:06:43,206 --> 00:06:45,400
the runner, and oranges.

195
00:06:45,580 --> 00:06:46,787
Number six.

196
00:06:46,967 --> 00:06:49,344
Bare branches in a tall vase,

197
00:06:49,344 --> 00:06:50,737
for a side table,

198
00:06:50,737 --> 00:06:52,459
not the dinner table.

199
00:06:52,639 --> 00:06:55,109
Find a branch with a pretty shape,

200
00:06:55,109 --> 00:06:57,289
birch or dogwood or even a red

201
00:06:57,289 --> 00:06:58,525
twig from a bush.

202
00:06:58,705 --> 00:07:01,018
Stand it in a big jar of

203
00:07:01,018 --> 00:07:01,596
rocks.

204
00:07:01,776 --> 00:07:04,519
Hang a few small ornaments or paper

205
00:07:04,519 --> 00:07:05,851
snowflakes on it.

206
00:07:06,031 --> 00:07:08,620
It's a little Christmas tree for nothing.

207
00:07:08,970 --> 00:07:11,169
And here's a trick for any of

208
00:07:11,169 --> 00:07:12,534
these, sweetheart.

209
00:07:12,714 --> 00:07:15,162
Use what you already own in a

210
00:07:15,162 --> 00:07:15,837
new way.

211
00:07:16,017 --> 00:07:18,594
A teapot with a sprig of holly.

212
00:07:18,774 --> 00:07:21,212
An old pair of ice skates hung

213
00:07:21,212 --> 00:07:22,025
on a hook,

214
00:07:22,025 --> 00:07:23,732
filled with greenery.

215
00:07:23,912 --> 00:07:26,389
A wooden sled leaned against the porch

216
00:07:26,389 --> 00:07:27,302
with a wreath.

217
00:07:27,482 --> 00:07:29,744
An old tin pail of pinecones on

218
00:07:29,744 --> 00:07:30,547
the hearth.

219
00:07:30,727 --> 00:07:33,545
The best decorations aren't the ones you

220
00:07:33,545 --> 00:07:33,827
buy.

221
00:07:34,007 --> 00:07:36,062
They're the ones with a story.

222
00:07:36,412 --> 00:07:37,642
Number seven.

223
00:07:37,822 --> 00:07:39,459
The cake stand trick.

224
00:07:39,639 --> 00:07:41,845
Put a pillar candle in the center

225
00:07:41,845 --> 00:07:43,650
of a cake stand or a pretty

226
00:07:43,650 --> 00:07:44,051
plate.

227
00:07:44,231 --> 00:07:46,133
Circle it with greenery,

228
00:07:46,133 --> 00:07:48,828
then pinecones, then a ring of red

229
00:07:48,828 --> 00:07:50,651
berries or cranberries.

230
00:07:50,831 --> 00:07:53,036
Raising it up on the cake stand

231
00:07:53,036 --> 00:07:55,882
makes even simple things look important.

232
00:07:56,062 --> 00:07:59,360
I use my grandmother's milk glass cake

233
00:07:59,360 --> 00:07:59,881
stand.

234
00:08:00,061 --> 00:08:02,511
People think it came from a fancy

235
00:08:02,511 --> 00:08:02,883
shop.

236
00:08:03,233 --> 00:08:04,289
Number eight.

237
00:08:04,469 --> 00:08:06,749
A wreath laid flat on the table,

238
00:08:06,749 --> 00:08:08,672
with candles in the middle.

239
00:08:08,852 --> 00:08:10,317
Take a small wreath,

240
00:08:10,317 --> 00:08:12,882
homemade or the cheapest one at the

241
00:08:12,882 --> 00:08:14,275
store, lay it flat,

242
00:08:14,275 --> 00:08:17,353
and set three candles of different heights

243
00:08:17,353 --> 00:08:18,379
in the center,

244
00:08:18,379 --> 00:08:19,185
on a plate.

245
00:08:19,365 --> 00:08:21,853
The wreath frames the candles like a

246
00:08:21,853 --> 00:08:22,407
picture.

247
00:08:22,587 --> 00:08:24,667
And when Christmas is over,

248
00:08:24,667 --> 00:08:27,056
it goes right back on the door.

249
00:08:27,236 --> 00:08:29,454
One wreath, two jobs.

250
00:08:29,804 --> 00:08:31,051
And number nine,

251
00:08:31,051 --> 00:08:32,845
the one I promised you.

252
00:08:33,025 --> 00:08:35,823
My mother's Christmas dinner centerpiece.

253
00:08:36,003 --> 00:08:37,368
She took her big,

254
00:08:37,368 --> 00:08:39,295
flat wooden bread board,

255
00:08:39,295 --> 00:08:40,098
and on it,

256
00:08:40,098 --> 00:08:42,667
she built a little winter scene.

257
00:08:42,847 --> 00:08:45,216
Moss from the woods for the ground.

258
00:08:45,396 --> 00:08:49,094
Small pine branches stuck upright like little

259
00:08:49,094 --> 00:08:49,587
trees.

260
00:08:49,767 --> 00:08:51,021
Pinecones.

261
00:08:51,201 --> 00:08:52,385
Little rocks.

262
00:08:52,565 --> 00:08:55,332
A tiny wooden church that my father

263
00:08:55,332 --> 00:08:55,886
carved.

264
00:08:56,066 --> 00:08:58,586
And a little mirror for a frozen

265
00:08:58,586 --> 00:08:58,980
pond.

266
00:08:59,330 --> 00:09:01,955
She sprinkled it with a little flour

267
00:09:01,955 --> 00:09:04,069
for snow, and set a few small

268
00:09:04,069 --> 00:09:05,819
candles around the edge.

269
00:09:05,999 --> 00:09:08,792
When the lights went down at Christmas

270
00:09:08,792 --> 00:09:11,144
dinner and the candles were lit,

271
00:09:11,144 --> 00:09:13,496
it looked like a tiny village in

272
00:09:13,496 --> 00:09:14,672
the snow, honey.

273
00:09:14,852 --> 00:09:17,466
The minister's wife saw it one year

274
00:09:17,466 --> 00:09:19,706
and asked to borrow it for the

275
00:09:19,706 --> 00:09:21,946
church altar on Christmas Eve.

276
00:09:22,126 --> 00:09:23,925
My mother said yes.

277
00:09:24,105 --> 00:09:26,504
She was proud as a peacock all

278
00:09:26,504 --> 00:09:26,903
week.

279
00:09:27,254 --> 00:09:28,725
And after Christmas,

280
00:09:28,725 --> 00:09:31,742
honey, don't just throw all that greenery

281
00:09:31,742 --> 00:09:32,699
in the trash.

282
00:09:32,879 --> 00:09:35,832
Lay the branches over your flower beds

283
00:09:35,832 --> 00:09:38,786
outside, to protect the roots from the

284
00:09:38,786 --> 00:09:40,263
hard January frost.

285
00:09:40,443 --> 00:09:42,766
Put the pinecones in a basket by

286
00:09:42,766 --> 00:09:44,727
the fireplace for kindling.

287
00:09:44,907 --> 00:09:47,714
And save the pretty dried berries and

288
00:09:47,714 --> 00:09:50,065
seed pods in a shoebox for next

289
00:09:50,065 --> 00:09:50,445
year.

290
00:09:50,625 --> 00:09:54,398
My mother's shoebox of Christmas pods lasted

291
00:09:54,398 --> 00:09:55,512
twenty years.

292
00:09:55,862 --> 00:09:57,418
So one more time.

293
00:09:57,598 --> 00:09:58,603
Keep it low,

294
00:09:58,603 --> 00:10:00,779
no taller than your elbow.

295
00:10:00,959 --> 00:10:04,257
Soak your greenery overnight and mist it.

296
00:10:04,437 --> 00:10:06,505
Pick three materials,

297
00:10:06,505 --> 00:10:07,293
not ten.

298
00:10:07,473 --> 00:10:09,365
Then a pinecone bowl,

299
00:10:09,365 --> 00:10:10,357
a Yule log,

300
00:10:10,357 --> 00:10:13,061
mason jars, a greenery runner,

301
00:10:13,061 --> 00:10:14,774
oranges and cloves,

302
00:10:14,774 --> 00:10:17,297
bare branches, a cake stand,

303
00:10:17,297 --> 00:10:18,559
a flat wreath,

304
00:10:18,559 --> 00:10:20,813
or a little winter scene.

305
00:10:20,993 --> 00:10:22,921
All from your backyard,

306
00:10:22,921 --> 00:10:25,520
sweetheart, for almost nothing.

307
00:10:25,870 --> 00:10:27,747
Tell me in the comments,

308
00:10:27,747 --> 00:10:28,216
honey.

309
00:10:28,396 --> 00:10:31,089
What's growing in your backyard that you

310
00:10:31,089 --> 00:10:31,763
could use?

311
00:10:31,943 --> 00:10:33,990
I'd love to see what you come

312
00:10:33,990 --> 00:10:34,555
up with.

313
00:10:34,735 --> 00:10:37,857
The whole playlist of Grandma's Christmas tips

314
00:10:37,857 --> 00:10:39,893
is right there on your screen,

315
00:10:39,893 --> 00:10:41,793
and I'll see you in the next

316
00:10:41,793 --> 00:10:42,200
video.

317
00:10:42,380 --> 00:10:44,284
Good night, sweetheart.
